<div class="wp-block-group ifm-panel ifm-takeaways"><!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Key points</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list {"className":"ifm-ticks"} -->
<ul class="wp-block-list ifm-ticks"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Your Google Business Profile is free and decides much of what people see when they search for you nearby.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Categories, services and opening hours matter more than anything else you fill in.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Keep your name, address and phone number identical everywhere online.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Ask real customers for reviews. Never buy them or offer rewards.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>When someone searches for a business like yours nearby, Google usually shows a map with three businesses above the normal results. The information in that box comes from your Google Business Profile. It is free, and for most local trades it brings more calls than the website does.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>This checklist covers the twelve things worth getting right, in the order we work through them on a <a href="/services/local-seo/">Local SEO</a> order.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Claim and verify the profile</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Search for your business name on Google Maps. If a listing exists, claim it. If not, create one at business.google.com. Google will then ask you to prove the business is yours, most often by recording a short video of your premises, signage or tools, and sometimes by phone or postcard. Only the owner can do this step.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Get the basics exactly right</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Business name:</strong> use your real trading name. Adding extra words such as your town or service breaks Google's rules and can get a profile suspended.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Address or service area:</strong> if customers visit you, show the address. If you travel to them, hide it and list the towns or postcodes you cover.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Phone number:</strong> use a number that is answered in working hours.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Opening hours:</strong> keep them accurate, and add special hours for bank holidays.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Website:</strong> link to the most relevant page, which is not always the home page.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Choose the right categories</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Your primary category is the strongest signal you give Google about what you do. Pick the most specific one that fits: "Emergency plumber" beats "Plumber" if emergencies are your main work. Then add secondary categories for your other real services. Do not add categories for work you do not offer.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">List your services and write the description</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Add each service you offer, with a short description. Then write the business description. You have up to 750 characters. Say what you do, where you do it and what makes you a sensible choice, in plain words. The first two sentences matter most because the rest is often hidden behind "More".</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"ifm-inline-cta"} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ifm-inline-cta"><!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Rather have this done for you?</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Our Google Business Profile Optimisation package covers every step on this page, with a report showing what was changed.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:buttons -->
<div class="wp-block-buttons"><!-- wp:button -->
<div class="wp-block-button"><a class="wp-block-button__link wp-element-button" href="/services/local-seo/#packages">See Local SEO packages</a></div>
<!-- /wp:button --></div>
<!-- /wp:buttons --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Add photos that show real work</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Upload your logo, a cover photo and pictures of your premises, your team at work and finished jobs. Real photos, even from a phone, do better than stock images. Add new ones regularly, since a profile with recent photos looks like a business that is open.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Keep your details the same everywhere</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Google compares your profile with what it finds on directories such as Yell, Bing Places and Apple Maps, and on your own website. If the name, address or phone number differ, it trusts them less. Decide on one exact version and use it everywhere. This is what a citation or directory listing package does.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Ask for reviews the right way</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Reviews influence both where you appear and whether people call. Ask every happy customer, soon after the job, and send them the direct link from your profile. Reply to every review, including the bad ones, calmly and briefly.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Do not offer discounts or gifts for reviews, do not ask only the customers you think will be positive, and never buy reviews. All three break Google's rules, and fake reviews are also against UK consumer law.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Post updates</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Google lets you publish short posts on your profile: an offer, a finished job, a seasonal reminder. One a week is plenty. Posts show customers you are active and give Google fresh information about what you do.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Check it every month</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Profiles drift. Customers suggest edits, hours change, photos get added by the public. Once a month, log in, check the details, answer new reviews and look at the performance figures to see which searches are finding you.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"ifm-faq","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ifm-faq"><!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Is Google Business Profile free?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Yes. Creating, verifying and managing a profile costs nothing. Be wary of anyone who phones claiming to be from Google and asking for payment to keep your listing.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>How long does verification take?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Video verification is usually reviewed within a few working days. Postcards take longer. Your profile may not be fully visible until verification is complete.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Can I have a profile without a shop or office?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Yes. If you travel to your customers, set the profile up as a service-area business and hide your address.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Why has my profile been suspended?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The most common reasons are extra keywords in the business name, an address that is not a real place of business, or duplicate listings. Fix the cause before asking Google to reinstate it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

